body:
I think the main use case for providing a set of previews would be to allow automatic fallback to different _types_ of previews, rather than responsive image selection.

For example, `blurhash()` or something similar might be added in the future, but you want to provide a solid `color()` fallback for people using browsers which do not support blurhash.

A full-css solution would handle this automatically using standard CSS behaviour. To extend the example in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/13176#issuecomment-7016500145 you could repeat the `content:` property, and the browser would ignore the declarations which use unsupported content types.

```html
<img src="https://…" style="content: rgb(123 45 67); content: blurhash(…)">
```

or, if a selector is needed that means it can't be done inline:

```html
<img src="https://…" data-preview-color="rgb(123 45 67)" data-preview-blurhash="…">
```

combined with CSS that pulls values from the data attributes:

```css
img[data-preview-color]:loading {
  content: attr(data-preview-color type(<color>));
}
img[data-preview-blurhash]:loading {
  /* ignored if browser doesn't support blurhash() */
  content: blurhash(attr(data-preview-blurhash type(<string>)));
}
```
